normalized_answer:
Yes. The Discussions GraphQL API is shipped and available.

To list your community communications on your website, query the repository’s `discussions` connection and request the fields you need, such as `title`, `bodyHTML` or `bodyText`, `url`, `createdAt`, and `updatedAt`. Discussion replies are available through each discussion’s `comments` connection; comments likewise expose `bodyHTML` and `bodyText`.

Illustrative query shape:

```graphql
query {
  repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPOSITORY") {
    discussions(first: 10, orderBy: {field: UPDATED_AT, direction: DESC}) {
      nodes {
        number
        title
        bodyHTML
        bodyText
        url
        createdAt
        updatedAt
        comments(first: 20) {
          nodes {
            bodyHTML
            bodyText
            createdAt
            url
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The connection supports pagination with `after`, `before`, `first`, and `last`, and can filter by `categoryId` or `answered`. The API is available to authenticated users, OAuth apps, and GitHub Apps. Tokens require `repo` for private repositories or `public_repo` for public repositories. See the local document “Using the GraphQL API for Discussions” for the complete schema and operation details.

