normalized_answer:
Use a nested `connectOrCreate` array on `categories`. Each item either connects the category identified by a unique field or creates it if that category does not exist.

Because the shown schema has `id` as the unique identifier and `name` is not marked `@unique`, identify categories by `id`. For newly created categories, provide their IDs explicitly so the same identifier can be used in both `where` and `create`:

```ts
await prisma.brand.create({
  data: {
    name: "Samsung",
    categories: {
      connectOrCreate: [
        // Existing category
        {
          where: { id: "existing-category-id-1" },
          create: { name: "unused if the category already exists" },
        },
        {
          where: { id: "existing-category-id-2" },
          create: { name: "unused if the category already exists" },
        },

        // New categories
        {
          where: { id: "new-category-id-1" },
          create: {
            id: "new-category-id-1",
            name: "Phones",
          },
        },
        {
          where: { id: "new-category-id-2" },
          create: {
            id: "new-category-id-2",
            name: "Electronics",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  },
});
```

For each entry, Prisma connects the existing category when the `where` record is found; otherwise it creates the related category using `create` and connects it to the new brand. The `connectOrCreate` relation field accepts an array, so multiple existing and new categories can be handled within the same `brand.create` operation.

