Yes. Your `lib/prisma.js` file is the appropriate place to register Prisma middleware because it is where the shared `PrismaClient` instance is created or retrieved. Add `prisma.$use(...)` after the production/development initialization logic and before `export default prisma`.

This is Prisma Client middleware, so it runs around Prisma database operations made through that client. It is separate from Next.js 12 request middleware, which concerns incoming HTTP requests. The exact callback depends on the operation you want to intercept; the supported placement is:

```js
// after the existing PrismaClient initialization
prisma.$use(yourPrismaMiddleware);

export default prisma;
```

Register it once in this shared module rather than separately in every API route. The Prisma Client reference identifies `$use` as a client-level method; if you later use an extended client, `$use` must be called before extending it.

