Use Prisma’s `include` option on the `create` call to return the related User object in the result. The `userId` foreign-key field will remain present; `include` adds the related `user` relation to the returned record.

Illustrative shape (use your actual relation field name):

```ts
const commentInstance = await prisma.comment.create({
  data: {
    comment,
    userId: user.id,
    cars_modificationId: modification.id,
  },
  include: {
    user: true,
  },
})
```

If you want to connect by the User relation instead of assigning the foreign key directly, use a nested `connect` in `data`, then still use `include` to return the User:

```ts
// Illustrative shape
const commentInstance = await prisma.comment.create({
  data: {
    comment,
    user: { connect: { id: userId } },
    cars_modification: { connect: { id: modificationId } },
  },
  include: { user: true },
})
```

The relation field may be named differently in your Prisma schema. `connect` associates the new record with an existing User, while `include` controls which related records are returned.

