normalized_answer:
Tailwind cannot generate `bg-[${item.background}]` from a template string. It scans your source as plain text and needs to find the complete utility class during the build. The runtime value `bg-[#aabbcc]` is not present as a complete class, so Tailwind emits the class attribute but not its CSS rule. That is why DevTools shows the class without a corresponding style.

For a finite set of allowed colors, map each value to a complete, statically written class:

```jsx
const backgroundClasses = {
  red: "bg-[#ff0000]",
  blue: "bg-[#0000ff]",
  neutral: "bg-[#aabbcc]",
};

// Select a key, then use:
<div className={`${backgroundClasses[selectedColor]} ...`} />
```

Every possible utility must appear in full in the source. The local Tailwind documentation also describes `@source inline()` as a way to explicitly generate specific utilities that are not present in scanned files; this is suitable when the set of classes is known in advance.

For arbitrary hexadecimal values entered at runtime, Tailwind alone cannot discover and generate every possible `bg-[#hex]` utility from the template string. In that case, the supported solution from the accepted answer is to apply the user-selected value as an inline background style rather than constructing a Tailwind class. If inline styles are not allowed, restrict the picker to a finite set of colors and use the static mapping approach.

