Register `.heading-1` as a component rather than a utility. In the plugin, replace `addUtilities(newUtilities)` with `addComponents(newUtilities)`:

```js
plugin(function ({ addComponents }) {
  const newComponents = {
    '.heading-1': {
      fontSize: '27px',
      fontStyle: 'normal',
      fontWeight: '600',
      lineHeight: 'normal',
      textTransform: 'capitalize',
      color: colors.secondary.gray,
      [`@media screen and (min-width: ${BREAKPOINTS.lg}px)`]: {
        fontSize: '40px',
      },
    },
  };

  addComponents(newComponents);
});
```

Then a utility can override an individual property when both classes are present:

```jsx
<p className="heading-1 text-blue-500">Hello World</p>
```

The `components` layer is intended for more complex, reusable classes that should remain overridable by utility classes, whereas custom utilities are added to the utilities layer alongside the built-in utilities.

