normalized_answer:
Yes—safelist the classes that may appear in the HTML returned by Hono, rather than adding shim elements to the Astro markup.

In the CSS source where Tailwind is imported, use `@source inline(...)` to explicitly generate those utilities:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";

@source inline("{hover:,}bg-red-{50,{100..900..100},950}");
```

The documented example generates the matching `bg-red-*` classes and their `hover:` variants. For your application, provide the complete server-side class names in the `@source inline(...)` source, or use brace expansion when the classes follow a predictable pattern. Tailwind scans source as plain text, so dynamically assembled fragments such as `bg-${color}-500` are not enough; the complete class names must be present.

