normalized_answer:
Your custom `animation` and `keyframes` entries are likely replacing Tailwind’s default theme values rather than extending them. That removes the definitions used by the preloaded `animate-bounce` utility, so the class can remain in the markup without Tailwind generating the corresponding animation rules.

Place the custom entries inside `theme.extend`, rather than directly inside `theme`:

```ts
// tailwind.config.ts
export default {
  theme: {
    extend: {
      animation: {
        shimmer: "shimmer 2s infinite",
      },
      keyframes: {
        shimmer: {
          "100%": {
            transform: "translateX(100%)",
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
};
```

The important part is the `extend` nesting. This preserves Tailwind’s built-in animation and keyframe definitions while adding `shimmer`. The accepted resolution identifies overriding the defaults by not using `extend`; the relevant documentation topic is “Extending the default theme.” Because the complete configuration was not provided, this diagnosis assumes the shown entries are directly under `theme` rather than already under `theme.extend`.

