The values are being read correctly; the problem is the way the Tailwind class names are constructed. In your code, these expressions build class names from fragments:

- `gap-${style.gap}`
- `h-${style.divSize}` and the other size utilities
- `text-${style.text}`

For example, Tailwind sees `text-` and `2xl` separately, not the complete `text-2xl` class. Tailwind scans source files as plain text and cannot understand interpolation or string concatenation, so it will not generate CSS for dynamically assembled classes. This can make the styling appear inconsistent.

Use complete class names in the mapping instead. Illustrative shape:

```jsx
const childStyle = {
  sm: {
    gap: "gap-2",
    size: "h-32 max-h-32 min-h-32 w-32 min-w-32 max-w-32",
    text: "text-2xl",
  },
  md: {
    gap: "gap-5",
    size: "h-60 max-h-60 min-h-60 w-60 min-w-60 max-w-60",
    text: "text-4xl",
  },
  lg: {
    gap: "gap-10",
    size: "h-80 max-h-80 min-h-80 w-80 min-w-80 max-w-80",
    text: "text-7xl",
  },
};

const style = childStyle[type];

// Use the mapped complete class strings:
// className={`... ${style.gap}`}
// className={`... ${style.size}`}
// className={style.text}
```

The important part is that every possible Tailwind utility—such as `gap-2`, `h-32`, and `text-2xl`—appears as a complete string in the source. Interpolating a variable that selects a complete mapped class is fine; interpolating pieces of a class name is not.

