normalized_answer:
This is expected behavior, not a problem with the shown `tailwindFunctions` configuration or your `tw` implementation.

The formatter cannot statically analyze a complete class name when interpolation occurs inside it, such as `bg-${color}-600`. As a result, it sorts the statically readable portions of the template independently; it cannot sort classes across the interpolation.

Tailwind has the same static-analysis limitation: it scans source files as plain text and cannot determine which complete classes will be produced by runtime string interpolation. Avoid constructing class names dynamically. Instead, map each possible value to a complete class name that exists in the source:

```ts
const backgroundClasses = {
  blue: "bg-blue-600",
  red: "bg-red-600",
};

const myClass = tw`${backgroundClasses[color]} py-1 px-1`;
```

This makes `bg-blue-600` and `bg-red-600` statically detectable by Tailwind. It does not, based on the available evidence, guarantee that Prettier will sort classes across the interpolation; the plugin's segment-by-segment behavior is the expected limitation.

