normalized_answer:
`text-${color}-600` is built at runtime, but Tailwind scans your source as plain text during the build. It does not evaluate JavaScript interpolation, so it cannot discover that the resulting class should be `text-blue-600` and therefore does not generate its CSS.

For a fixed set of possible colors, map each value to a complete class name:

```jsx
export default function Home() {
  const color = "blue";

  const textColors = {
    blue: "text-blue-600",
    red: "text-red-600",
  };

  return <p className={textColors[color]}>This is a test</p>;
}
```

Every complete class—such as `text-blue-600` and `text-red-600`—is then present in the source for Tailwind to detect. Since your example always uses blue, simply writing `className="text-blue-600"` also solves it.

Alternatively, explicitly register or safelist every possible complete color class in your Tailwind setup. The exact registration syntax depends on your Tailwind version; the relevant procedure is Tailwind’s class-detection documentation under “Safelisting with ranges.”

