normalized_answer:
In Tailwind CSS v4, the guidance is to implement most new extensions directly in CSS, using directives such as `@utility` and `@custom-variant`. The v3-style JavaScript plugin mechanism was not removed entirely: existing v3 plugins can still be loaded with the `@plugin` directive, for example:

```css
@plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";
```

`@plugin` accepts either a package name or a local path. The current documentation describes this under the compatibility section, as a way to load legacy JavaScript-based plugins while incrementally moving custom utilities, variants, themes, and related configuration to CSS.

Thus, for a new third-party extension, use the CSS-driven APIs where possible; use `@plugin` when you need to consume an existing v3-compatible JavaScript plugin. The supplied documentation confirms compatibility for loading legacy plugins, but does not specify a separate v4 workflow for authoring new JavaScript third-party plugins.

The issue is most likely an unlayered CSS reset overriding Tailwind’s padding and margin utilities. In Tailwind v4, put your reset rules in the `base` layer so the utility classes can override them normally.

Update your main CSS file to use this structure:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";

@layer base {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  * {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  html {
    font-family: sans-serif;
    color: #333;
  }
}
```

Remove the same reset rules if they also exist outside `@layer base`. Then you can remove the `!` modifiers:

```html
<div class="text-[clamp(1.9rem,_3.5vw,_5.25rem)] text-center pt-[160px] pb-[80px] mx-[40px]">
  The first human-quality AI agent
</div>
```

The `!` version works because it forces those utilities to take precedence over the competing reset rules. Moving the reset into `@layer base` gives the normal Tailwind utilities the intended precedence instead. This is the approach described in Tailwind’s “Adding custom styles” documentation under “Adding base styles.”

