normalized_answer:
Use `--foreground` as a regular CSS custom property outside `@theme`, and override it in the selector that represents the condition (for example, a dark-mode class or data attribute). Keep the theme reference inline:

```css
@theme inline {
  --color-background: var(--background);
  --color-foreground: var(--foreground);
}

:root {
  --background: /* light background value */;
  --foreground: /* light foreground value */;
}

.dark {
  --background: /* dark background value */;
  --foreground: /* dark foreground value */;
}
```

The `:root`/`.dark` portion is an illustrative CSS shape; replace `.dark` with whatever selector or condition your application uses.

The important detail is `@theme inline`: when a theme variable references another variable, `inline` makes generated utilities use the referenced value directly. Without it, the reference can be resolved where the theme variable is defined, before a more deeply scoped `--foreground` value is available. Theme variables compile to regular CSS variables, so the referenced `--foreground` can be defined or overridden outside the `@theme` block.

