Yes. In this setup, the SCSS reset is winning over the Tailwind utility, as shown by `.text-brand-primary` being struck through in the browser’s CSS inspector. The direct fix is to put the reset styles in Tailwind’s `base` layer:

```scss
// global/theme.scss
@layer base {
  a,
  input,
  button,
  select,
  textarea {
    font-family: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }
}
```

Keep loading `global/theme.scss` from `global.scss` as you do currently. With the reset in `base`, the `text-brand-primary` utility can take precedence from Tailwind’s utilities layer.

Also note that your component explicitly sets `color: inherit` on hover:

```scss
button:hover {
  color: inherit;
}
```

Therefore, if the incorrect color occurs only while hovering, remove that rule or change it; it intentionally overrides the button’s text color. More generally, Tailwind v4 is designed to replace the Sass preprocessing workflow, so avoid Sass-specific processing where possible and use Tailwind’s CSS imports, layers, native nesting, and CSS variables instead.

