normalized_answer:
Yes. This is an expected Tailwind CSS v4 change. In v3, classes written inside `@layer components` or `@layer utilities` were picked up as Tailwind utility classes, so variants such as `hover:`, `focus:`, and responsive variants were generated automatically. In v4, `@layer` uses native CSS cascade layers and is no longer treated specially by Tailwind.

Register the class with the `@utility` API instead:

```css
@utility btn-success {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  color: rgb(var(--c-white) / 1);
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  background-color: red;
  transition: opacity 250ms ease-in-out;
}
```

You can then use the registered utility with variants, for example:

```html
<button class="hover:btn-success focus:btn-success">
  Save
</button>
```

Thus, the issue is not that `hover` or `focus` must be written differently inside `@layer components`; rather, the class must be registered as a Tailwind custom utility with `@utility` if you want Tailwind to generate variant forms for it. The same applies to classes previously placed in `@layer utilities`.

