Yes. Define `my-glass` as a Tailwind custom utility with `@utility`, rather than as an ordinary CSS class:

```css
@utility my-glass {
  /* your glass-effect declarations */
}
```

You can then use it with the dark variant:

```js
const activeClass =
  'bg-white border-black ring ring-stone-700/50 dark:my-glass';
```

The important distinction is that `@utility` registers the class in Tailwind’s utilities layer, allowing Tailwind variants to target it. This lets you keep the light-mode styling as ordinary utilities and apply the more complex custom utility only in dark mode.

If `my-glass` is instead defined as ordinary custom CSS, apply the variant inside that rule using `@variant dark`:

```css
.my-glass {
  /* light/default declarations */

  @variant dark {
    /* dark-mode declarations */
  }
}
```

For the approach in your example, the recommended solution is therefore to register `my-glass` with `@utility` and continue using `dark:my-glass`.

