Tailwind v4 does not need the Rails Tailwind gem or Sprockets for this. The relevant setup is Tailwind’s source detection in the CSS entrypoint used by Vite.

Tailwind scans source files as plain text, so your `.erb` files can be scanned for complete class names. Explicitly register the Rails views directory with `@source`, using a path relative to the stylesheet that imports Tailwind:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";
@source "<relative-path-to-your-Rails-views>";
```

For example, replace the placeholder with the relative path from your CSS file to `app/views` (the exact number of `..` segments depends on where that CSS file is located). The documented operator is `@source`; it belongs in the Tailwind CSS file, rather than as a Vite-plugin option.

This addresses scanning ERB for Tailwind classes. Ensure the class names appear in full in the ERB source—Tailwind cannot detect class names assembled through interpolation or string concatenation. The `assets` error is consistent with attempting to use Rails/Sprockets-oriented integration in an application that does not configure Sprockets; that integration is not required for the Vite-based Tailwind build.

