Define custom variants with the existing names and add a selector branch for the scoped override. Keep the media-query behavior as the default, while allowing an ancestor with `data-ignore` to activate the same `motion-safe:` or `motion-reduce:` utilities:

```css
@custom-variant motion-safe {
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    @slot;
  }

  &:where([data-ignore], [data-ignore] *) {
    @slot;
  }
}

@custom-variant motion-reduce {
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    @slot;
  }

  &:where([data-ignore], [data-ignore] *) {
    @slot;
  }
}
```

You can then place `data-ignore` on a product or app container:

```html
<div data-ignore>
  <!-- Existing motion-safe:* and motion-reduce:* classes remain unchanged. -->
</div>
```

The `data-ignore` branch makes both variants apply within that subtree, regardless of the system preference. This preserves the existing variant names and media-query behavior while adding a product- or preference-controlled scope. The same attribute could be set from an in-app preference, but because overriding reduced motion can cause discomfort for some users, it should be an explicit, reversible choice rather than an implicit assumption based on a user's role or environment.

