normalized_answer:
In Tailwind CSS v4, define theme values in your CSS with `@theme` instead of using a JavaScript configuration file.

**Custom colors**

Add this to the CSS file that imports Tailwind:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";

@theme {
  --color-midnight: #121063;
  --color-tahiti: #3ab7bf;
  --color-bermuda: #78dcca;
}
```

You can then use the generated utilities:

```html
<div class="bg-midnight text-tahiti fill-bermuda"></div>
```

The `--color-*` namespace creates color utilities such as `bg-*`, `text-*`, `border-*`, `fill-*`, and others. You can also override an existing color by defining a theme variable with the same name.

**Custom fonts**

Define a font family in the same CSS file:

```css
@theme {
  --font-display: "Oswald", sans-serif;
}
```

Use it with the corresponding utility:

```html
<h1 class="font-display">Heading</h1>
```

If the font is a local file, load it with `@font-face` before defining the theme variable:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: Oswald;
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 200 700;
  font-display: swap;
  src: url("/fonts/Oswald.woff2") format("woff2");
}

@theme {
  --font-display: "Oswald", sans-serif;
}
```

For a hosted font, put its URL `@import` at the very top of the CSS file, before `@import "tailwindcss"`, then map the family through `@theme`.

Declaring a custom color is not presented as a requirement for large projects. It is the Tailwind v4 mechanism for giving project colors stable names and generating reusable utilities; use it when your project has a shared or custom palette. You can also disable the default palette and define only your own colors with `--color-*: initial`.

