normalized_answer:
Tailwind cannot evaluate the AlpineJS template literal. The standalone compiler scans files as plain text rather than executing AlpineJS or parsing JavaScript, so the complete class names `text-[#26BBE3]`, `text-[#4CD7C1]`, and `text-[#F7847B]` do not exist in the interpolated expression at build time. [Dynamic class names](docs/tailwind-css/src/docs/detecting-classes-in-source-files.mdx)

Use one of these approaches:

1. **Safelist the exact arbitrary-value utilities in your input CSS** using `@source inline()`:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";

@source inline("text-[#26BBE3]");
@source inline("text-[#4CD7C1]");
@source inline("text-[#F7847B]");
```

Then your Alpine expression can remain:

```html
<div x-text="item.category"
     :class="`text-sm text-[#${item.color}]`"></div>
```

This is the CSS equivalent of your hidden elements, but it keeps the safelist in the compiler input rather than the HTML.

2. **Map each possible value to a complete class name** in JavaScript instead of constructing the class name:

```html
<script>
  const textColors = {
    "26BBE3": "text-[#26BBE3]",
    "4CD7C1": "text-[#4CD7C1]",
    "F7847B": "text-[#F7847B]",
  };
</script>

<div x-data="{ reasons: reasons, textColors: textColors }">
  <template x-for="(item, index) in reasons">
    <div x-text="item.category"
         :class="`text-sm ${textColors[item.color]}`"></div>
  </template>
</div>
```

The important distinction is that each full utility appears literally in a scanned source file. This follows Tailwind's documented pattern of mapping values to complete, statically detectable class names rather than interpolating pieces of a class name. The hidden `div` approach also works for the same reason, but `@source inline()` or a static map is generally cleaner. [Safelisting specific utilities](docs/tailwind-css/src/docs/detecting-classes-in-source-files.mdx)

