Configure the UI package’s Tailwind import to scan the UI components explicitly:

```css
/* packages/ui/src/globals.css */
@import "tailwindcss" prefix(ui) source("./components");
@import "./variables.css";
@import "tw-animate-css";
```

The `source("./components")` setting makes Tailwind scan `packages/ui/src/components`, where the `ui:*` class names are used. This is especially relevant in a monorepo because Tailwind’s default scan base is the current working directory, which may be the repository root rather than the UI package.

Keep the application stylesheet unprefixed so classes written directly in the app continue to work:

```css
/* apps/web/src/globals.css */
@import "tailwindcss";
@plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";
```

Then ensure the UI stylesheet itself is imported by the application, rather than importing only `@acme/ui/variables.css`. In other words, the app needs to include the UI package’s `globals.css` as well as its own `globals.css`. The two stylesheet imports should be kept separate if importing both Tailwind entry points into one pipeline causes conflicts; the accepted setup uses an import of `@acme/ui/src/globals.css` alongside the app’s `./globals.css` import.

With that arrangement, the UI stylesheet generates the `ui:`-prefixed utilities used by `Button`, while the app stylesheet generates ordinary utilities such as `bg-blue-500` and `p-4`. Keep class names complete and statically present in the component source; Tailwind scans source as text and does not resolve dynamically constructed class names.

